.Dd September 27, 2026
.Dt PRLIMIT64 2
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm prlimit64 ,
.Nm getrlimit ,
.Nm setrlimit ,
.Nm ugetrlimit
.Nd get or set process resource limits
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Fd #define _GNU_SOURCE
.In sys/resource.h
.Ft int
.Fn getrlimit "int resource" "struct rlimit *rlim"
.Ft int
.Fn setrlimit "int resource" "const struct rlimit *rlim"
.Ft int
.Fn prlimit "pid_t pid" "int resource" "const struct rlimit *new_limit" "struct rlimit *old_limit"
.Fd #define prlimit64 prlimit
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Fn getrlimit
reads a resource's limits for the calling process.
.Fn setrlimit
sets them.
Each limit has a soft value,
.Va rlim_cur ,
and a hard value,
.Va rlim_max ,
in
.Vt struct rlimit .
Musl declares both fields as the 64-bit type
.Vt rlim_t .
.Dv RLIM_INFINITY
denotes no limit.
.Pp
With
.Dv _GNU_SOURCE
defined, musl declares
.Fn prlimit
and defines
.Nm prlimit64
as a macro for it.
.Fa pid
selects the process, by its ID or that of any of its threads; zero
selects the caller's.
The limits are the process's, and a change applies to all its threads.
Another process may be selected by a caller with effective user ID 0
or the same real user ID as that process.
If
.Fa new_limit
is non-null, it supplies new values.
If
.Fa old_limit
is non-null, it receives the values from before the attempted change.
Either or both pointers may be null.
.Pp
Six resources are stored and enforced:
.Bl -tag -width RLIMIT_NOFILE
.It Dv RLIMIT_NPROC
The number of threads with the same real user ID across the machine, as
Linux counts them: each thread of a process counts, and one that has
ended counts until it is reaped.
.Xr fork 2 ,
.Xr vfork 2
and
.Xr clone 2 ,
a new thread's included, return
.Er EAGAIN
when the caller's soft limit is reached.
Real user ID 0 is exempt.
A change of real user ID moves the thread to the new user's count.
.It Dv RLIMIT_NOFILE
One greater than the highest descriptor number the process may receive.
The hard limit cannot exceed 1024, even for root.
Open returns
.Er EMFILE
when no descriptor below the soft limit is free.
Dup2 returns
.Er EBADF
for a destination at or above the soft limit.
A process that shares its descriptor table with another, as
.Xr clone 2
with
.Dv CLONE_FILES
makes them, is held to its own limit in it, as on Linux: one sharer's
lower limit does not bind the others, and a higher one does not free them
from theirs.
.It Dv RLIMIT_AS
The bytes covered by the process's virtual memory regions.
Growth beyond the soft limit makes mmap return
.Er ENOMEM ,
leaves the break unchanged in brk, and prevents stack growth.
.It Dv RLIMIT_STACK
The bytes of the stack.
.Xr execve 2
gives the new image a stack that may grow until it is the soft limit's
size in whole pages, and no further than
.Ad 0xB0000000 ,
below which anonymous mappings are placed; a fault past that is
.Dv SIGSEGV .
The stack always reaches as far as the pages its arguments took.
A quarter of the soft limit, between 128 KiB and 6 MiB, is what
.Xr execve 2
lets the arguments and the environment take.
A change applies at the next
.Xr execve 2 .
An exec that runs with an effective ID its caller lacks lowers a soft
limit above 8 MiB to 8 MiB, as Linux's does.
.It Dv RLIMIT_CORE
The bytes of a core file, holes excepted; a soft limit below a page
leaves none, as
.Xr core 5
describes.
.It Dv RLIMIT_FSIZE
The bytes a regular file may reach through
.Xr write 2 ,
.Xr pwrite64 2 ,
.Xr writev 2 ,
.Xr truncate 2
and
.Xr ftruncate 2 :
a write that would pass the limit writes what fits below it, and a write
that starts at it, or a truncation past it, fails with
.Er EFBIG
and sends the calling thread
.Dv SIGXFSZ ,
which ends the process with a core file unless it is caught or ignored,
as on Linux.
.El
.Pp
For these resources the soft limit must not exceed the hard limit.
A process may lower its hard limits; only a caller with effective user
ID 0 may raise a hard limit.
Fork inherits both values and exec preserves them.
The kernel's initial soft and hard limits are 4096 processes,
1024 descriptors, and unlimited address space; the stack's are 8 MiB
soft and unlimited hard, the core file's 0 soft and unlimited hard, and a
file's size unlimited, which are Linux's.
Init may replace these defaults as described in
.Xr limits 7 .
.Pp
Every other resource number, including an unknown number, reports
unlimited values.
Setting any resource other than the six stored resources succeeds
without changing anything, even if its soft value exceeds its hard value.
.Pp
Linux measures each growth of the stack against the limit in force when
it happens, so a process that raises its own
.Dv RLIMIT_STACK
may grow its stack further at once; here the limit of the exec that
started the image applies until the next exec.
.Pp
The kernel stores limits in 32 bits.
A 64-bit value with a nonzero high word, or the value 4294967295,
is treated as unlimited.
An unlimited value is returned with all 64 bits set.
.Pp
The i386 system call
.Nm prlimit64
is number 340.
Musl uses it for
.Fn prlimit ,
.Fn getrlimit ,
and
.Fn setrlimit .
The older calls
.Nm getrlimit
(76),
.Nm setrlimit
(75), and
.Nm ugetrlimit
(191) use two unsigned 32-bit fields, with 4294967295 as infinity.
Musl has no separate
.Fn ugetrlimit
declaration; its getrlimit fallback uses that kernel number on i386.
.Pp
The kernel copies new values in, records the previous values, attempts
the change, then copies the previous values out.
An output-copy failure can therefore return
.Er EFAULT
after changing the limits.
The previous values are copied out after the change is attempted, so a
valid old-limit buffer receives them when the change is refused too; a
failure to read the new limits, to find the target or in the permission
check returns before the buffer is written.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
These functions return 0 on success, or -1 on failure and set
.Va errno .
The raw kernel calls return a negative error number on failure.
.Sh EXAMPLES
The default suite's
.Pa tests/limitprobe.c
sets the descriptor limit to eight, checks that open returns
.Er EMFILE
when those descriptors are occupied, and checks that dup2 refuses a
destination at or above eight with
.Er EBADF .
It also checks process and address-space limits, inheritance, and the
privilege required to raise a hard limit.
.Pa tests/musl/stackmusl.c
checks the stack limit's default, inheritance and refusals, and that an
exec lets the stack grow to 12 MiB under a limit of 16 MiB and kills it
with
.Dv SIGSEGV
past a limit of 256 KiB.
.Sh ERRORS
.Bl -tag -width EINVAL
.It Er EFAULT
A required limit buffer cannot be read or written.
.It Er EINVAL
For a stored resource, the new soft limit exceeds the new hard limit.
.It Er EPERM
A caller without effective user ID 0 tries to raise a stored hard limit
or select a process with a different real user ID.
The requested descriptor hard limit exceeds 1024.
Selecting a process record that is unused or a zombie also gives this
error.
.It Er ESRCH
No process record exists for
.Fa pid .
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr brk 2 ,
.Xr dup 2 ,
.Xr fork 2 ,
.Xr mmap 2 ,
.Xr open 2 ,
.Xr core 5 ,
.Xr limits 7
